+"""nn::audio on the Linux host (NX2Linux S7bis): the audio renderer and the audio output as the game's client API sees them. Every call that would reach the audio services is replaced; the objects are tracked by the guest
+address of their opaque struct. The renderer's system event is signalled every `sampleCount / sampleRate` seconds (the game's audio thread waits on it), memory pools attach at once, and what the game plays is MIXED:
+each tick a started voice resamples its wave buffers (linear, the voice's rate and pitch) into the buses its mix volumes name, the device sink's inputs make the output channels (a 5.1 sink is downmixed to stereo), and the
+block goes to libnx2linux_audio.so (an SDL3 device, or the file NX2LINUX_AUDIO=wav:<file> names, see horizon_mix.py). A wave buffer is released when the voice has played it. PCM 8-bit, 16-bit and float voices are mixed; ADPCM (Nintendo's DSP-ADPCM, 16 predictor coefficients from the parameter given when the voice is acquired) is decoded when a wave buffer is queued; a format that is not decoded leaves the voice silent, counted and logged once. Audio-out buffers (PCM 16-bit stereo) are mixed in the same output and released as they are played. Layouts used: AudioRendererParameter {u32 sampleRate;
+u32 sampleCount}, WaveBuffer {ptr; size; s32 startSampleOffset; s32 endSampleOffset; bool loop} (the SDK keeps nothing else in the first 25 bytes), AudioOutBuffer {ptr; capacity; size}."""
